    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so if the CNS is the issue, how much of a reduction in volume are we talking about for a deload? like should i just cut the sets in half or keep the intensity high but just do less work? i want to make sure they actually recover without losing the strength gains they've already made.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For a deload, we typically reduce the training volume by roughly 50% to 60% while maintaining high intensity, usually keeping the weight heavy or even at 90%+ of their previous max. This allows you to preserve the strength gained from the current block while giving your CNS sufficient time to recover from the accumulated fatigue. Adding a few low-intensity mobility or activation sessions on the deload day can further support recovery without discouraging effort.",
      "source": "target",
