      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Plateaus often feel sudden because they result from cumulative fatigue masking specific neural adaptations or肌腱 stiffness that can't be repaired with standard rest alone. Physiologically, this happens when central nervous system drive drops below the threshold needed to recruit high-threshold motor units, even if your peripheral muscles have adapted. It isn't random; it's a natural feedback loop where the body temporarily halts progression to force a shift in stress management or recovery environment before growth resumes.",
